c(46, "§7.1 table (verbatim); §7.1; §7.2 table (verbatim); §7.2", "timeline", "Six eras set from the product timeline (verbatim): " + table("## 7.1 Method", 0) + " — review volume per month collapses 45× from E1 to E6; the mean falls monotonically across five eras then ticks up on 13 reviews. Theme movement (verbatim): " + table("## 7.2 Theme movement", 0) + " — praise 77.87% → 33.9%; defects 12.36% → 50.85% (a majority of E5 reviews report something broken); abandonment claims 0.62% → 20.34%; PR_DESIGN never collapses (15.25% even in E5): 'The app never stopped being beautiful. It stopped working.'", "n/a", "mixed", "E1 809 / 59.1 per month / 4.16★; E2 473 / 4.14; E3 308 / 3.59; E4 123 / 3.51; E5 59 / 1.7 per month / 3.07; E6 13 / 3.38", "none", "table", "app-specific", ["9271340335", "3827656315"], "", "")

c(47, "§7.3; §7.3 Wave 1; §7.3 Wave 2; §7.3 Wave 3; §7.3 Wave 4; §7.3 Wave 5", "timeline", "The five crash waves: (1) launch, March 2016 — shipped as an Apple Editors' Choice (MKT_EDITORS 6) and crashed immediately ('Great crash simulator… All it does is crash'), 21 crash reviews in the first three weeks; with the one-habit paywall, the worst month in the app's history — 209 reviews at 3.11★, 54 on 2016-03-11 alone — leaving 228 reviews (12.77% of the corpus) at 3.25★ permanently on the listing; (2) iOS 9/10, Oct–Nov 2016 — 18 launch-crash reviews ('I'd advise iOS 9 users to think carefully before buying'); (3) Face ID / Touch ID, March 2018 — enabling the biometric lock, a paid feature, crashed the app on entry ('What was my 30 yuan for?… I appealed to Apple and was told no refund'), the same fortnight as the purchase outage — 'the month this app broke its paying users on two axes at once'; (4) iOS 12 / watchOS 5, Sep–Oct 2018 — the Watch app broken by the OS update ('App hasn't had an update in nearly 2 months, what's going on guys?'); (5) the black screen, November 2022 — 1.9.63 shipped 2022-11-09, the first black-screen review the same day, 14 in the following year ('I love this app and have years of check-in records, so I really want this fixed'), and the next release 1,030 days later.", "n/a", "1★-burst", "wave 1: 21 crash reviews, 2016-03 209 reviews / 3.11★; wave 2: 18; wave 3: 5 in 9 days; wave 4: 7; wave 5: 14 then 1,030-day gap", "must-never-break", "High-priority", "app-specific", ["1345892603", "1346499392", "1470204089", "1486760895", "2273762627", "3226036476", "9272101069", "9295872427"], "", "an Editors' Choice launch that crashes pins its first month's reviews to the listing for a decade")

c(48, "§7.4", "timeline", "The 2018 redesign — rejected, then rolled back: in November 2018 the check-in and calendar UI changed (NEG_REDESIGN 23, 3.22★) — 'Instead of a single tap to log a habit for the day, you're forced to go through multiple steps now!' (1★); the calendar mark shrank 'from a fully filled-in square to a TINY dot'; 'no visual indicator telling you that you have completed that habit… I have ended up clicking the same habit multiple times'; the developer rolled it back and reviewers upgraded their ratings in public: 'Dev Team reacted quite quick… Happy to have single tap check in back… Changed my rating to 5 stars ;-)' (edited 1★ → 5★); 'Thank you for listening! The little green dot… has been restored to a solid green dot' — 'the corpus's clearest demonstration of responsiveness converting anger into loyalty'.", "check-in redesign Nov 2018, rolled back within months", "mixed", "NEG_REDESIGN 23 / 3.22★; rollback confirmed by 2 edited reviews; FIX_CONFIRM 33", "product-rule", "Meaningful", "generalisable", ["3390321767", "3392908796", "3418021543", "3403476071", "3493496861", "3491619389"], "", "a rolled-back redesign converts a 1★ into a 5★ in the same review")

c(49, "§7.5; §8.5 reference", "timeline", "The widget: shipped, lost, restored — and never modernised: requested on day two of the app's life ('I use my today screen every day'), the Notification-Centre widget shipped October 2016 and was loved (PR_WIDGET 28, 4.50★; 'son Widget super bien pensé'; for many the primary interface — 'the main way i used the app'); removed silently in 1.9.55 (2021-09-02, notes say nothing about it) — 11 reviews in six languages ('No more widget. Which is the main way i used the app. So, I probably won't keep up to be using it', 1★; 'Give it back'; '还我小组件'); restored in 1.9.57 (2021-11-04) — 'Thank you for making the widget come back!'; but the iOS-14 home-screen widget (announced June 2020) was never built — requests from 2020-09-21 to 2025-12-15 ('I NEED a Today widget on my health/goals page'; 'The app needs a widget for IOS 26'), REQ_WIDGET rising from 1.98% of E1 to 16.95% of E5 — 'the only request that grows as the corpus shrinks'.", "NC widget 2016 → removed 2021 → restored; home-screen widget never", "mixed", "PR_WIDGET 28 / 4.50★; removed 11 reviews; REQ_WIDGET 47, 1.98% → 16.95%", "must-have", "Very strong", "generalisable", ["1345876517", "1470744014", "1476474960", "7774471535", "7786249554", "7895168833", "7939841492", "8022641382", "6552481616", "6453348017", "13304685452"], "", "")

c(50, "§7.6 table (verbatim); §7.6", "insight", "What improved and what it cost (verbatim): " + table("## 7.6 What improved", 0) + " — 'the pattern is latency, not absence': almost everything users asked for was eventually built or fixed — dark mode two years after the request, the time-zone check-in bug six and a half years after the first report, the performance fix after months of daily lag — and in several cases (iPad, home-screen widget, bad-habit tracking, archive, overview) never across ten years and 1,785 reviews.", "n/a", "mixed", "12 deliveries; median lag years; 5 never", "none", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["1351843491", "1470744014", "3491619389", "5535282962"], "", "delivery latency measured in years is functionally the same as absence for the reviewer who asked")

c(51, "§7.7 table (verbatim); §7.7; §7.8 tables (verbatim); §7.8", "data-caveat", "Persisted in every era (verbatim): " + table("## 7.7 What persisted", 0) + " — BUG_SWIPE (2016 → 2024), BUG_NOTIF (2016 → 2025) and REQ_DASHBOARD (2016 → 2026) each span essentially the entire corpus. Sensitivity (verbatim): " + table("## 7.8 Sensitivity", 0) + " || CIs (verbatim): " + table("## 7.8 Sensitivity", 1) + " — no single episode drives the decline: removing the botched launch raises the mean by 0.08, the 2022 black-screen wave by 0.01; the gap that matters is pre-2019 (4.13★) vs 2019-onward (3.52★) — 0.61 stars over 458 reviews and seven years, 'not a bad week; it is the product's trajectory'.", "n/a", "mixed", "pre-2019 4.13★ vs 2019+ 3.52★; launch exclusion +0.08; black-screen exclusion +0.01", "none", "table", "app-specific", ["1346490678", "1887236677"], "", "")

